## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable data lifecycle with multi-tenant governance and cost control; integrates Iceberg features, storage tiering, policy-driven retention, and query-layer masking.

## Key Concepts
- Iceberg time travel via snapshots
- Dynamic data tiering (hot/warm/cold)
- Per-tenant data masking
- Encryption at rest (KMS)
- Partition pruning and metadata caching

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectTier(tenant, ageDays) {
  const retention = (tenantRetention[tenant] || 365);
  if (ageDays < 30) return 'hot';
  if (ageDays < retention) return 'warm';
  return 'cold';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data freshness and mask correctness across tiers?
- How would you validate time travel remains accurate after tiering moves?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes privacy-preserving multi-tenant data architectures, lineage, and streaming trade-offs not covered by earlier questions.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Data contracts and schema drift
- Dynamic masking of PII
- GDPR delete and lineage preservation
- Iceberg lakehouse semantics
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: mask PII fields for non-admins
function maskPII(record, role) {
  if (role !== 'admin') {
    record.email = '***@***';
    record.phone = record.phone?.replace(/\\d/g, 'x');
  }
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant deletes across long-running streams?
- How would you test schema drift and masking policy changes in production?

## Why This Is Asked

Data governance for multiple data sources with contracts ensures safety, compatibility, and faster onboarding of teams at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and schema registry
- Versioned Iceberg schemas and per-source isolation
- Idempotent upserts and SCD2 history
- Contract testing and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-contract check: validate record against contract version
function validate(record, contract) {
  // check required fields
  for (const f of contract.required) {
    if (!(f in record)) return false;
  }
  // type checks
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(contract.types)) {
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect contract drift across sources, and what actions would you trigger?
- How would you handle data already ingested when a contract is updated—rollback, versioning, or retroactive validation?
